(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old woman is brought in after a car crash with a heart rate of 128 and a blood pressure of 88/56. The rhythm is regular with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Bedside ultrasound shows free fluid in the abdomen. Which is the most appropriate approach to her heart rate?","choices":["Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous metoprolol","Treat the bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A regular rhythm at 128 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus tachycardia, and with a blood pressure of 88/56 and free abdominal fluid after a crash it is an appropriate response to hypovolemia. Appropriate sinus tachycardia is managed by treating the underlying cause, here the bleeding. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has sudden palpitations after three energy drinks. She notices her neck throbbing, and examination shows a frog sign (rapid visible pulsations in the neck). Her heart rate is 172 and regular, with a narrow QRS and no visible P waves. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sudden, regular narrow tachycardia at 172 with hidden P waves, neck pulsations (frog sign) and a caffeine trigger in a young woman without heart disease is atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia. It is a reentry circuit within the node, far more common in women, and usually runs at 140 to 180. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has had 20 minutes of sudden palpitations. Her heart rate is 188, blood pressure 122/76, and she is alert with no chest pain or breathlessness. The electrocardiogram shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia with no visible P waves. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A sudden, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 188 with no visible P waves is supraventricular tachycardia, and with a blood pressure of 122/76, a clear mind and no chest pain or breathlessness she is stable. A vagal maneuver is first line, followed by adenosine, which may slow the rhythm enough to show P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man had coronary bypass surgery two months ago. He now has palpitations and fatigue. His heart rate is 150 and regular, and the electrocardiogram shows sawtooth waves, two for every narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular rate of 150 with two sawtooth waves for every narrow QRS is atrial flutter with 2:1 block, the most common ratio, in which the node blocks one of every two atrial impulses. Atrial scarring or damage from surgery, such as his recent bypass operation, is a common setting for flutter. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man had cardiac surgery last year. A rhythm strip shows normal sinus beats, then a sudden pause with no P wave, after which narrow beats at 45 appear with no visible P waves. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial flutter","Sinus arrest","Sinus bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normal sinus beats followed by a sudden pause with no P wave mean the sinus node stopped generating impulses: sinus arrest, a form of sick sinus syndrome. The escape rhythm is junctional, narrow beats at 45 with no visible P waves. Injury to the sinus node from cardiac surgery is a listed cause, matching his history. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has frequent premature atrial contractions on a monitor (early beats with abnormal P waves), many per minute. He has no known heart disease. Which evaluation is most appropriate?","choices":["No evaluation is needed","Look for structural heart disease","Test for an accessory pathway","Serum digoxin level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His premature atrial contractions are frequent, many per minute, and when they are frequent the step is to look for underlying structural heart disease. In patients with heart disease these beats may precede paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter, which makes the search worthwhile. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's frequent premature atrial contractions are shown on monitoring to trigger his episodes of atrial fibrillation. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Septal myectomy","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His premature atrial contractions are frequent and monitoring shows them triggering atrial fibrillation, the two settings in which catheter ablation is used. Otherwise, persistent symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has paroxysmal atrial fibrillation and no hypertension, diabetes, heart failure, stroke or vascular disease, so his CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score is 0. Which anticoagulation plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Lifelong direct oral anticoagulant","Heparin infusion at home","May not be needed","Warfarin, target ratio 2 to 3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke-risk) score is 0: age under 65 (0), male (0), and no heart failure, hypertension, stroke, vascular disease or diabetes. A score of 0 in a man, or 1 in a woman, is low risk and may not require anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman with Wolff-Parkinson-White has had three emergency visits for reentrant tachycardia this year. Which long-term management is first line?","choices":["Lifelong oral adenosine","Permanent pacemaker","Lifelong anticoagulation","Ablation of the pathway"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has Wolff-Parkinson-White with recurrent reentrant tachycardia, and for chronic management of Wolff-Parkinson-White, catheter ablation of the accessory pathway is the first-line treatment. Acute episodes otherwise rely on adenosine, which must never be used if atrial fibrillation with the pathway appears. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has had atrial fibrillation for years and asks why her clinician insists on a blood thinner. Which complication is the anticoagulant meant to prevent?","choices":["Stroke from a clot","Heart block","Ventricular fibrillation","A faster heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation in atrial fibrillation is about stroke risk, assessed with the CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke-risk) score, and patients typically require it unless bleeding risk is high. At 79 she scores 2 points for age 75 or older and 1 for female sex, so a blood thinner is expected. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a slow sinus rhythm at 34 is hypotensive and drowsy. Two doses of atropine have not changed his rate or blood pressure. Which is the next step?","choices":["Temporary pacemaker","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Oral metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A sinus rhythm at 34 with hypotension and drowsiness is acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia, and atropine, the first-line drug, has failed after two doses. The next step is a temporary pacemaker; a permanent pacemaker is for chronic symptomatic bradycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is told his electrocardiogram shows a normal P wave before each narrow QRS at 68 beats per minute, with the rate rising as he breathes in and falling as he breathes out. He worries that he has heart disease. Which counseling is most accurate?","choices":["He needs an anticoagulant","A normal variant; no treatment","He needs a pacemaker workup","He should stop exercising"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A normal P wave before each narrow QRS at 68, with the rate rising on inspiration and falling on expiration, is sinus arrhythmia. It is a normal, physiologic rhythm, irregular with a pattern at 60 to 100, so he can be reassured that it is not heart disease and needs no treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man has known tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) and asks which fast rhythm is behind his palpitations. An ambulatory monitor is arranged. Which rhythm is it most likely to record during the fast episodes?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Junctional rhythm","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which a fast rhythm alternates with slow rates or pauses, the tachyarrhythmia is most commonly atrial fibrillation. During his fast episodes the monitor would most likely show an irregularly irregular rhythm without P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has atrial flutter (sawtooth flutter waves) with a ventricular rate of 140. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Adenosine twice","Permanent pacemaker","A beta or calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sawtooth flutter waves with a ventricular rate of 140 in a woman with right-sided heart failure are atrial flutter, and she is alert with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain, so she is stable. Stable flutter is rate controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker, though control is harder to achieve than in atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is admitted with an inferior myocardial infarction. His heart rate falls to 46 with narrow QRS complexes and P waves that are either inverted (retrograde P waves) or buried in the QRS. Which rhythm is most likely?","choices":["Junctional rhythm","Sinus bradycardia","Atrial flutter","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular, narrow-complex rhythm at 46 with P waves inverted or buried in the QRS means the impulse starts in the atrioventricular (AV) node: a junctional rhythm, usually 40 to 60. When the low node fires first the P wave is retrograde, after the QRS; mid-node firing buries it. Inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man has a junctional rhythm at 42 with dizziness. Monitoring shows that the cause is high-grade atrioventricular block. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Observation","Adenosine","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm at 42 with dizziness, caused by high-grade atrioventricular block, needs a permanent pacemaker. Management of junctional rhythm depends on the cause, and complete or high-grade block or sick sinus syndrome is the setting for a pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is brought in confused, with a blood pressure of 76/40. The monitor shows a slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus bradycardia, and confusion with a blood pressure of 76/40 make it acute, symptomatic and hemodynamically unstable. Atropine is first line; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has new fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance and constipation. Her pulse is 50 and regular, with a normal P wave before each narrow QRS. She takes no medications. Which test is most useful next?","choices":["Thyroid function tests","Serum digoxin level","Electrophysiology study","Ambulatory monitor for pauses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular rate of 50 with a normal P wave before each narrow QRS is sinus bradycardia, and with no medications the drug causes are excluded. Hypothyroidism is a listed cause, and her fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance and constipation point to it, so thyroid testing comes next. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman notices occasional skipped beats during a stressful month of heavy coffee and wine. Her electrocardiogram shows occasional early beats with an abnormally shaped P wave and a narrow QRS, and her examination and echocardiogram are normal. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Start a beta blocker","Refer for ablation","Start anticoagulation","Reassure; avoid triggers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Occasional early beats with an abnormal P wave and a narrow QRS are premature atrial contractions, and in a normal heart they occur with excess alcohol, stress and caffeine, all present in her month. Management is to counsel avoidance of these precipitants and reassure her about the benign nature of the rhythm. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with hypertension and diabetes has nonvalvular atrial fibrillation and a high CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score. Which anticoagulant class is preferred?","choices":["Aspirin alone","No anticoagulant, given her age","A direct oral anticoagulant","Warfarin as first choice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke-risk) score is high (age 75 or older 2, female 1, hypertension 1, diabetes 1 = 5), so she typically requires anticoagulation unless bleeding risk is high. In nonvalvular atrial fibrillation the direct oral anticoagulants (dabigatran, apixaban, edoxaban or rivaroxaban) are preferred. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has focal atrial tachycardia at 165. He is hemodynamically stable but symptomatic with palpitations. Which acute treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Unsynchronized defibrillation","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous beta blocker","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He has focal atrial tachycardia at 165, hemodynamically stable but symptomatic with palpitations. That is treated with an intravenous beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker while the underlying cause and precipitating factors are identified and treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has frequent premature junctional contractions, more than 10 per minute, on a monitor. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Anticoagulation","Check for heart disease","Reassurance alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Premature junctional contractions that are frequent, more than 10 per minute or multifocal, prompt a search for underlying structural heart disease, and the beats are then treated with a beta blocker. Her rate of more than 10 per minute meets that threshold. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) on monitoring, and he faints during the pauses. Thyroid tests and electrolytes are normal and he takes no rate-slowing drugs. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Atropine as needed","Catheter ablation alone","Observation alone","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He faints during the pauses of tachy-brady syndrome, so his symptoms correlate with the bradycardia, and the reversible causes (thyroid disease, electrolyte abnormalities, rate-slowing drugs) have been excluded. Symptomatic bradycardia in sick sinus syndrome is treated with a permanent pacemaker, and tachy-brady syndrome often needs one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with hypertension, diabetes and obstructive sleep apnea has had palpitations for two days. His pulse is irregularly irregular at 132, and the electrocardiogram shows no P waves and a narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregularly irregular, narrow-complex rhythm at 132 with no P waves is atrial fibrillation, within its usual 110 to 160. Hypertension, diabetes, sleep apnea and older age, all present in this man, are among the conditions in which it is commonly seen. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man whose Wolff-Parkinson-White was found on a school electrocardiogram presents with palpitations. The rhythm is irregular and wide-complex, at rates up to 260, with varying delta waves (slurred upstrokes). He is alert with a blood pressure of 118/74. Which drug is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Adenosine","Intravenous magnesium","Atropine","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia up to 260 with varying delta waves in a man with known Wolff-Parkinson-White is atrial fibrillation conducting over the accessory pathway. When Wolff-Parkinson-White is known, intravenous procainamide is used, never adenosine; he is alert with a normal blood pressure, so a drug is appropriate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation at 150 has crackles throughout both lungs from pulmonary edema and a blood pressure of 82/50. Which feature makes this presentation a medical emergency?","choices":["Severe heart failure","A heart rate above 100","An irregularly irregular pulse","Her age over 65"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Crackles throughout both lungs from pulmonary edema and a blood pressure of 82/50 are severe heart failure, one of the listed medical emergencies in atrial fibrillation, along with atrial fibrillation during an acute myocardial infarction. Atrial fibrillation itself is usually not directly life-threatening. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has recurrent, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia despite an oral beta blocker. She wants a definitive option. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Lifelong anticoagulation","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Repeated adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her focal atrial tachycardia keeps recurring despite an oral beta blocker, the initial chronic therapy, and she wants a definitive option. Catheter ablation is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia, in about 90 percent. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 210. His blood pressure is 72/40, and he is confused and sweating. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Unsynchronized defibrillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 210 with a blood pressure of 72/40, confusion and sweating is hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachycardia, which gets immediate synchronized cardioversion: a timed shock at decreased voltage. Collapse like this is expected once the rate goes above 200, as his has. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman taking digoxin for heart failure has nausea and fatigue. Her heart rate is 48 and regular, with narrow QRS complexes and retrograde P waves (inverted) just after each QRS in II, III and aVF. Which is the most likely cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Digoxin toxicity","Hyperthyroidism","An accessory pathway","Missed digoxin doses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A slow, regular, narrow rhythm at 48 with inverted P waves just after each QRS in II, III and aVF is a junctional rhythm, and digoxin toxicity is a listed cause. Her nausea and fatigue while taking digoxin point to toxicity, which is treated with atropine and/or digoxin immune Fab (fragment antigen-binding). — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent atrioventricular nodal reentry has been taught to stop episodes at home with a vagal maneuver. Tonight an episode stopped within minutes after she did so, and she now feels well. What is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Arrange a pacemaker","No urgent care is usually needed","Come to the emergency department","Start an anticoagulant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She has been educated in vagal maneuvers, her episode of nodal reentry stopped within minutes after one, and she now feels well. When a vagal maneuver terminates nodal reentry at home in an educated patient, urgent medical attention is usually not required, since episodes are usually well tolerated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
